# 27. DEPLOYMENT STATE MACHINE

Deployment must support meaningful states.

Example:

Not Deployed
↓
Queued
↓
Building
↓
Deploying
↓
Deployed

Failure may occur during:

Building
↓
Failed

or:

Deploying
↓
Failed

The frontend must not assume:

Button clicked = deployed

Deployment completion must come from the backend/deployment system.

---

# 28. LIVE URL RULE

A live URL must only be displayed when a valid deployment result
has been received from the backend.

The frontend must never generate fake deployment URLs.

---

# 29. AUTHENTICATION ARCHITECTURE

Authentication must be centralized.

Preferred architecture:

Auth Provider
↓
Auth State
↓
Protected Route
↓
Application

Individual pages must not independently reinvent authentication logic.

---

# 30. AUTHENTICATION STATES

The frontend must correctly handle:

- Authenticated
- Unauthenticated
- Loading authentication
- Session expired
- Unauthorized
- Forbidden
- Network failure

The application must transition to an appropriate state
instead of leaving the user on a broken screen.

---

# 31. SESSION HANDLING

Session behavior must be predictable.

If authentication becomes invalid:

1. Detect the authentication failure.
2. Preserve appropriate application state where possible.
3. Prevent unauthorized operations.
4. Redirect or display the correct authentication experience
   according to the approved authentication architecture.

---

# 32. SECURITY PRINCIPLES

Client-side controls are not the final security boundary.

Examples:

Hidden button != Security

Disabled button != Authorization

Frontend route guard != Backend authorization

The backend must enforce:

- Authentication
- Authorization
- Ownership
- Permissions
- Access control

The frontend only provides the user experience around those rules.

---

# 33. PROJECT OWNERSHIP

When a project belongs to a user,
the frontend must rely on backend authorization to enforce ownership.

The frontend must not assume that possession of a project ID
grants access.

---

# 34. RESPONSIVE ENGINEERING

The application must support:

- Large Desktop
- Desktop
- Laptop
- Tablet
- Mobile

Responsive behavior must be intentional.

Desktop layouts must not simply overflow onto smaller screens.

---

# 35. RESPONSIVE PRIORITY

When screen space becomes limited:

1. Preserve primary actions.
2. Preserve core content.
3. Collapse secondary navigation where appropriate.
4. Maintain readable typography.
5. Maintain usable controls.
6. Avoid horizontal overflow unless intentionally designed.

---

# 36. ACCESSIBILITY

Interactive elements must support:

- Keyboard navigation
- Visible focus
- Semantic HTML
- Accessible labels
- Meaningful button names
- Appropriate ARIA
- Logical reading order
- Sufficient contrast

Critical information must never depend only on color.

Icons alone must not communicate critical actions
without accessible labels.

---

# 37. KEYBOARD BEHAVIOR

Common interactions should support expected keyboard behavior.

Examples:

- Enter submits appropriate forms.
- Shift + Enter creates a new line where applicable.
- Escape closes dialogs where appropriate.
- Tab navigates controls logically.
- Enter/Space activates appropriate controls.

Keyboard behavior must not conflict with text editing.

---

# 38. MODAL STANDARD

Modals are for focused decisions.

Examples:

- Delete Project
- Confirm Deployment
- Create API Key
- Upgrade Plan
- Confirm Account Action

A modal must not replace a full page when the content
requires substantial interaction.

---

# 39. DRAWER STANDARD

Drawers may be used for contextual information.

Examples:

- Project Details
- Activity
- Notifications
- Quick Settings

Primary navigation destinations remain full pages.

---

# 40. NOTIFICATION SYSTEM

ZyrionOS must use one consistent notification system.

Supported conceptual types:

- Success
- Error
- Warning
- Information

Notifications must use the approved design system.

Individual pages must not invent unrelated toast styles.

---

# 41. STATE MANAGEMENT

State should live at the lowest appropriate level.

Example:

Input state
→ Local component state

Workspace state
→ Workspace feature state

Authentication state
→ Global authentication layer

Application preferences
→ Appropriate global settings layer

Do not place every variable into global state.

---

# 42. SERVER DATA VS UI STATE

The frontend should distinguish between:

Server Data
and
UI State

Server data examples:

- Projects
- User profile
- Billing information
- Deployment status
- Usage information

UI state examples:

- Active tab
- Open modal
- Selected file
- Search input
- Sidebar state

This separation reduces unnecessary complexity.

---

# 43. ERROR BOUNDARIES

Major application areas should have controlled failure handling.

A failure inside one feature should not unnecessarily destroy
the entire application.

Example:

Workspace failure
!=
Entire Dashboard failure

---

# 44. PERFORMANCE STANDARD

The frontend must minimize unnecessary:

- Rerenders
- API requests
- Duplicate calculations
- Large component trees
- Unnecessary dependencies
- Oversized assets

Optimization must remain practical.

Avoid premature complexity without measurable benefit.

---

# 45. API REQUEST DISCIPLINE

The frontend must avoid accidental duplicate API calls.

Examples of problems to prevent:

- Multiple identical requests
- Requests triggered by unnecessary rerenders
- Requests after component unmount
- Repeated submissions
- Polling without clear purpose

---

# 46. DATA FRESHNESS

When backend data changes,
the frontend should update the relevant state intentionally.

Preferred approaches may include:

- Refetching
- Updating local state
- Invalidating cached data
- Receiving backend events

The selected method must remain consistent with the application architecture.

---

# 47. CODE QUALITY RULES

Production frontend code must maintain:

- Readable naming
- Predictable structure
- Small reusable components
- Minimal duplication
- Consistent imports
- Consistent formatting
- Clear responsibilities
- No dead code
- No unexplained magic values
- No random architecture decisions

---

# 48. FORBIDDEN FRONTEND PATTERNS

The following are prohibited in production:

- Fake project data
- Fake deployment URLs
- Fake success responses
- Hardcoded user information
- Random API endpoints
- Duplicate API clients
- Giant monolithic components
- UI-only deployment success
- Silent API failures
- Unhandled promise failures
- Production console errors
- Random colors outside the Design System
- Random spacing outside the Design System
- Random border radii
- Random button styles
- Duplicate versions of global components
- Business logic hidden inside presentation-only components
- Primary pages implemented as tiny widgets without approval

---

# 49. DESIGN SYSTEM ENFORCEMENT

Frontend implementation must consume the approved
ZyrionOS Design System.

Developers must not independently decide:

- Brand colors
- Typography
- Border radius
- Shadows
- Spacing
- Button appearance
- Input appearance
- Card appearance
- Badge appearance
- Modal appearance

If a required visual pattern does not exist,
the Design System must be updated according to the approved
design-system process instead of inventing a one-off solution.

---

# 50. AI CODING AGENT RULE

Any AI agent modifying the ZyrionOS frontend must follow:

1. Understand the existing structure.
2. Identify the affected page.
3. Identify the affected feature.
4. Identify reusable components.
5. Check the relevant service.
6. Check the API contract.
7. Check the Design System.
8. Implement only the required change.
9. Validate loading state.
10. Validate empty state.
11. Validate error state.
12. Validate success state.
13. Validate responsive behavior.
14. Validate accessibility.
15. Validate build.
16. Check for console errors.

AI agents must not rewrite unrelated files simply because
they prefer a different architecture.

---

# 51. AI AGENT CHANGE BOUNDARY

Before modifying a file, the AI coding agent must understand:

- Why the file is being changed.
- Which requirement requires the change.
- Which components depend on it.
- Which services it uses.
- Whether a reusable component already exists.

Unrelated refactoring must not be bundled into feature implementation.

---

# 52. CHANGE CONTROL

Approved product documentation is the source of truth.

Implementation flow:

Product Requirement
↓
Relevant Documentation
↓
Implementation
↓
Validation
↓
Production

If a requirement conflicts with an existing implementation,
